/* book: lead: A boost converter is a DC-DC circuit that makes a higher DC output from a lower DC input. It stores energy in an inductor from the source while a switch is on, then opens the switch so the inductor drives current through a diode into the output, in series with the source. The output is always above the input: Vo = Vs/(1 − D). Boost cells appear in battery-to-rail boosters, power-factor-correction  */
/* book Figure 3.4.1: The worked example’s boost: 24 V in, D = 0.6, 60 V out. The ideal gain 1/(1 − D) climbs without limit, but with only 1 Ω of winding resistance against the 100 Ω load the real gain peaks at 5 at D = 0.9 and then falls. */
